sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If an equilateral triangle is inscribed in a circle, then the square on the side of the triangle is triple the square on the radius of the circle.
----
proof:

Let {ABC|circT} be a circle, and let the equilateral triangle {ABC|BCA} be inscribed in it.

I say that the square on one side of the triangle {ABC|BCA} is triple the square on the radius of the circle.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="245" height="245" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="XIII.12 diagram" src="propXIII12.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's triangle BCA is already faceless, so the prose reaches the
    // triangle as {ABC|BCA}; his circle has a random face, which a highlight
    // would raise over the triangle (#140), so the prose reaches the circle
    // through circT, a faceless twin, as {ABC|circT}.
    //
    // Transparent targets: the sides AB, the radius pieces AD, DE of AE, and
    // the circumferences BEC and BE as arcs. BE has no lettered point
    // between its ends, so Xbe (uncoloured) is cut off on the ray from the
    // centre D through the midpoint of BE at the radius.
    //
    // Joyce's AE and BE are held back until "join AD and carry it through to
    // E, and join BE", and drawn there. The angle ABE is right (III.31,
    // cited with I.47): a red square at B, revealed where the squares on
    // AB and BE are summed.
    //
    // Draggables (checked against the bundle): B and C, free — the given
    // triangle's base, standing from slide 1. A, D, E are derived and listed
    // per slide; nothing to defer.
    var GIVEN = ["circ","circT","BCAdup","BCA","A","B","C","AB"];
    var CEN   = GIVEN.concat(["D"]);
    var JOIN  = CEN.concat(["AE","BE","E","AD","DE","arcBEC","arcBE"]);
    var RT    = JOIN.concat(["angABE"]);
    geomlib.init({
