</script>
</figure>

Take the center {D} of the circle {ABC|circT}, join {AD} and carry it through to {E}, and join {BE}. [!just III.1]

Then, since the triangle {ABC|BCA} is equilateral, therefore the circumference {BEC|arcBEC} is a third part of the circumference of the circle {ABC|circT}.  Therefore the circumference {BE|arcBE} is a sixth part of the circumference of the circle. Therefore the straight line {BE} belongs to a hexagon. Therefore it equals the radius {DE}. [!just IV.15.Cor]

And, since {AE} is double {DE}, therefore the square on {AE} is quadruple the square on {ED}, that is, of the square on {BE}.

But the square on {AE} equals the sum of the squares on {AB} and {BE}.  Therefore the sum of the squares on {AB} and {BE} is quadruple the square on {BE}. [!just III.31; I.47]

Therefore, taken separately, the square on {AB} is triple the square on {BE}. But {BE} equals {DE}, therefore the square on {AB} is triple the square on {DE}.

Therefore the square on the side of the triangle is triple the square on the radius.

Therefore, *if an equilateral triangle is inscribed in a circle, then the square on the side of the triangle is triple the square on the radius of the circle.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

#### Use of this proposition

This result is needed in proposition @XIII.13 coming next to show that the construction given there produces a regular tetrahedron.
